WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:53.983
- Hi, everyone.

00:00:53.983 --> 00:00:56.860
Welcome to the Daily Homeroom live stream.

00:00:56.860 --> 00:00:59.310
For those of y'all who are
wondering what this is,

00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:02.620
when we started having
physical school closures,

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:05.030
we realized, when everyone
had to be socially distanced.

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:07.150
We realized that it's our duty really

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:08.590
as a not-for-profit with a mission

00:01:08.590 --> 00:01:10.890
of free world class
education for anyone anywhere

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:13.220
to not only accelerate the development

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:15.170
of a lot of the software
tools and the content

00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:17.300
but also try to create as
many supports as possible

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:19.340
for parents, teachers and students

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:21.290
as we go through this fairly tough time

00:01:21.290 --> 00:01:23.700
and we've been running webinars
for teachers and parents

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:24.920
to understand how to use the different

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:27.050
Khan Academy resources and
we've been doing other things

00:01:27.050 --> 00:01:28.730
but we thought it'd be fun
to have a way to connect

00:01:28.730 --> 00:01:30.940
and stay synchronous
and have conversations

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:33.350
during this time of social distancing.

00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:35.160
So welcome to this live stream.

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:37.860
I do like to make my
standard announcement.

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:39.750
Khan Academy is a not-for-profit.

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:43.190
We only can exist because
of philanthropic donations

00:01:43.190 --> 00:01:44.330
from folks like yourself.

00:01:44.330 --> 00:01:46.800
I wanna give a special shout
out to several corporations

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:49.980
that have stepped up in the
last few weeks to help us,

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:51.680
especially with the COVID response.

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:54.150
We were running at a deficit
even before the crisis

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:55.950
and our costs have gone up.

00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:58.900
We're seeing about 2.5, 3X
the traffic we normally do.

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:00.900
We're trying to accelerate
a bunch of programs,

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:04.590
so special thanks to
Google, to Bank of America,

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:07.220
Google.org, AT&amp;T, Fastly and Novartis

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:09.040
and to the many other philanthropic

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:12.340
and corporate supporters
pre-crisis as well,

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:13.920
who continue to support us

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:16.090
and special thanks to the
many thousands of people

00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:17.987
who donate whatever they can,

00:02:17.987 --> 00:02:20.890
$3, $5, it all makes a difference

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:23.384
and it all adds up because
it allows us to serve

00:02:23.384 --> 00:02:26.670
many millions of students.

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:29.210
So today we've been having
some incredible guests

00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:31.030
and I'm sorry to announce
that today's guest

00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:33.820
is not so impressive, it's me.

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:35.450
So (laughs)

00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:39.100
every now and then, we like
to mix in having guests

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:42.320
and just a time for you to ask me anything

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:43.970
you would like to ask me

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:46.350
and I say that in the
broadest possible terms.

00:02:46.350 --> 00:02:48.110
I have yet to be offended by a question,

00:02:48.110 --> 00:02:50.320
although I don't wanna
encourage you too much

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:54.200
but please on YouTube and
Facebook, ask me anything

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:55.800
and we have team members who are looking

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:58.360
at the message boards on
both YouTube and Facebook

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:00.510
to surface any questions.

00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:03.400
And so already there's one from Facebook,

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:07.380
Cassava Raman asks, "Hi, Sal,
please answer my questions."

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:09.317
Well, I am answering it right now.

00:03:09.317 --> 00:03:11.937
"How did you study and how
did you recommend studying?

00:03:11.937 --> 00:03:15.180
"Please give a comparison
on effectiveness."

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:18.300
Well, I'll tell you what
that worked for me growing up

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:20.547
and even today, a lot of people ask me,

00:03:20.547 --> 00:03:24.137
"Sal, how do you make a
lesson on organic chemistry

00:03:24.137 --> 00:03:25.617
"and a lesson on the Napoleonic Wars

00:03:25.617 --> 00:03:27.367
"and a lesson on Physics?"

00:03:28.570 --> 00:03:31.980
Some of those subjects,
I have a deep intuition

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:34.150
and I know from my academic background

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:37.090
but a lot of them I have
to study to learn well.

00:03:37.090 --> 00:03:38.910
And so whether it was when I was in school

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:40.840
or even now when I'm studying,

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:43.330
I have a very active process.

00:03:43.330 --> 00:03:46.430
When I'm doing one, I like
if I'm doing something,

00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:48.380
especially in math or the sciences,

00:03:48.380 --> 00:03:50.650
I like to work through problems

00:03:50.650 --> 00:03:54.120
and sometimes it's problems
I can find in a textbook

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:55.610
or on say, Khan Academy

00:03:55.610 --> 00:03:58.380
and sometimes it's problems
I'm coming up with myself.

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:01.120
I was like, well, exactly,
how does a lead to b?

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:04.150
So my number one thing is
while you are studying,

00:04:04.150 --> 00:04:06.070
constantly ask yourself questions

00:04:06.070 --> 00:04:08.300
and even in a subject matter like history

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:09.380
where you say, okay, I have to read

00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:12.360
this fairly dense chapter
of my history textbook

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:15.760
to understand or to get a
sense of the context of things,

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:17.440
constantly asking yourself questions

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:21.850
like why did Julius
Caesar cross the Rubicon?

00:04:21.850 --> 00:04:23.380
Did he really think he could do it?

00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:24.720
And what that does is one,

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:27.320
it forces connections...

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:29.470
Frankly, it just makes
everything more interesting

00:04:29.470 --> 00:04:31.360
but you're also going to retain a lot more

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:35.130
because you're engaging more
of your brain on that concept.

00:04:35.130 --> 00:04:36.680
So it's not just about memorizing

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:39.450
Julius Caesar crossed the
Rubicon in such and such a date

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:41.630
but when you start to
put yourself in his shoes

00:04:41.630 --> 00:04:45.750
and say, okay, what's
the risk reward here?

00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:48.560
Would I have done the same
thing in that situation

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:49.830
or if you're in biology class,

00:04:49.830 --> 00:04:51.040
well, why does that make sense?

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:53.740
Or just even experiencing the awe of it.

00:04:53.740 --> 00:04:55.600
When you study the cell and you realize

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:57.703
it's almost like a whole
civilization in there,

00:04:57.703 --> 00:05:00.760
it's so much complexity and there's so...

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:03.650
It puts you in awe of how
everything all works together

00:05:03.650 --> 00:05:06.990
and I think when you study
with that type of a mindset,

00:05:06.990 --> 00:05:08.940
not only is the studying more effective

00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:12.810
but it is more enjoyable.

00:05:12.810 --> 00:05:14.870
I would say one key though is to not do it

00:05:14.870 --> 00:05:16.980
in a stressed state and to do that,

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:18.320
it's all about building a habit

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:21.240
so that you're able to learn consistently

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:24.120
and do, depending on your age
level or what you're studying,

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:26.220
20, 30, 40 minutes every day,

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:28.090
try to make it as active as possible

00:05:28.090 --> 00:05:31.050
and then what you see is by
the time you get to a test,

00:05:31.050 --> 00:05:33.693
it'll feel intuitive
versus if you cram it,

00:05:33.693 --> 00:05:37.440
six hours, the night before the exam.

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:39.250
And even if you're
studying on Khan Academy,

00:05:39.250 --> 00:05:40.950
if you're watching a video,

00:05:40.950 --> 00:05:43.870
always try to pause the
video, try to do it yourself,

00:05:43.870 --> 00:05:45.520
whether it's me or someone
else making the video

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:46.970
before we work through it.

00:05:46.970 --> 00:05:48.550
Try to see whether you could even come up

00:05:48.550 --> 00:05:51.080
with some of the intuitions
about it before we explain it

00:05:51.080 --> 00:05:53.910
and even if you do, you
watch the whole video,

00:05:53.910 --> 00:05:56.050
then stop and say, okay, let
me see if I can replicate

00:05:56.050 --> 00:05:56.960
that thought process.

00:05:56.960 --> 00:05:58.460
Let me see if I can explain it now.

00:05:58.460 --> 00:06:01.640
Let me see if I can go through
that same worked example.

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:03.950
So the more active you
make it, the better.

00:06:03.950 --> 00:06:08.407
See, other questions from
Emmanuel Peter asks from YouTube,

00:06:08.407 --> 00:06:11.210
"Are you going to put a geography
course on Khan Academy?"

00:06:11.210 --> 00:06:13.940
We don't, unfortunately
have near term plans

00:06:13.940 --> 00:06:15.780
to do a geography course, Emmanuel.

00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:17.700
I would love to eventually.

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:20.430
The vision of Khan Academy
is all of the core subjects.

00:06:20.430 --> 00:06:23.070
We have Khan Academy kids
now, so really from pre-K

00:06:23.070 --> 00:06:24.810
through elementary,
middle school, high school

00:06:24.810 --> 00:06:27.900
and even the core college but
we do not have geography yet,

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:29.960
which I think is a very valuable course.

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:32.963
So hopefully one day
some of it is resources,

00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:36.730
any philanthropists or
corporate sponsors out there

00:06:36.730 --> 00:06:40.100
or individual donors, things
like middle school science,

00:06:40.100 --> 00:06:42.670
geography, middle school
science is actually

00:06:42.670 --> 00:06:44.160
one of the areas that we would really like

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:46.670
to find some supports for if we can.

00:06:46.670 --> 00:06:48.680
We're starting to do stuff
in English and language arts

00:06:48.680 --> 00:06:50.980
for grades two through eight
but that's another area

00:06:50.980 --> 00:06:54.310
where we would love to
talk to potential sponsors

00:06:54.310 --> 00:06:55.980
and the Khan Academy Kids Effort,

00:06:55.980 --> 00:06:57.950
there was just this efficacy
study that came out.

00:06:57.950 --> 00:06:59.460
It was a randomized controlled trial,

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:02.710
which means they randomly put students

00:07:02.710 --> 00:07:04.230
into one of two groups,

00:07:04.230 --> 00:07:06.530
the control group or the treatment group

00:07:06.530 --> 00:07:08.490
and in the control group,

00:07:08.490 --> 00:07:10.890
they used a kind of a family friendly app.

00:07:10.890 --> 00:07:12.770
These are five-year olds, four-year olds

00:07:12.770 --> 00:07:15.350
who come from high poverty families

00:07:15.350 --> 00:07:17.980
and then the treatment group,
they used Khan Academy kids,

00:07:17.980 --> 00:07:20.240
20 minutes a day and the treatment group

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:22.260
after I think it was
about a month and a half,

00:07:22.260 --> 00:07:24.540
they were completely, they
were able to close the gap

00:07:24.540 --> 00:07:27.520
between some of these really
high-need communities,

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:30.550
these high-need students and
their more affluent peers.

00:07:30.550 --> 00:07:32.620
So these students went from
roughly the 30th percentile

00:07:32.620 --> 00:07:34.200
to the 50th percentile
in a week and a half.

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:37.740
So that's another area that
I would love to add more

00:07:37.740 --> 00:07:39.240
if we can find the resources.

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:40.840
It goes up to first
grade, I would love to add

00:07:40.840 --> 00:07:44.240
to second grade, third grade
and then internationalized

00:07:44.240 --> 00:07:46.490
but no geography just yet.

00:07:46.490 --> 00:07:49.767
Okay, from Facebook Asmar Avil asks,

00:07:49.767 --> 00:07:52.230
"Hi, I'm new to using
Khan Academy Kids app."

00:07:52.230 --> 00:07:54.163
Oh, well, we were just talking about that.

00:07:54.163 --> 00:07:58.007
" or Khan Academy app, do you
have both both apps in Arabic?

00:07:58.007 --> 00:07:59.307
"I did a quick search and I found

00:07:59.307 --> 00:08:00.567
"you have a website in Arabic

00:08:00.567 --> 00:08:01.557
"but I actually couldn't find it.

00:08:01.557 --> 00:08:03.660
"Any clarification as
much is appreciated."

00:08:03.660 --> 00:08:05.970
So thanks for asking Asmar.

00:08:05.970 --> 00:08:10.970
So unfortunately we do not
have either app in Arabic yet.

00:08:13.140 --> 00:08:16.150
We do have many localization
projects around the world.

00:08:16.150 --> 00:08:19.020
You can imagine though,
languages like Arabic

00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:23.180
and Farsi and Hebrew and Urdu,

00:08:23.180 --> 00:08:24.360
they are right to left

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:26.020
and sometimes bi-directional languages

00:08:26.020 --> 00:08:29.640
and we haven't been able to
build that into our product yet.

00:08:29.640 --> 00:08:32.380
That is one of our to-dos
that we hope to be able to do

00:08:32.380 --> 00:08:33.330
one day.

00:08:33.330 --> 00:08:36.940
With that said, there
is a translation project

00:08:36.940 --> 00:08:38.700
of Khan Academy into Arabic

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:39.910
and I think if you do Google search,

00:08:39.910 --> 00:08:42.910
you should be able to find
that translation project.

00:08:42.910 --> 00:08:44.600
So it's not the full functionality,

00:08:44.600 --> 00:08:47.190
it's not the exercises and all of that

00:08:47.190 --> 00:08:49.530
but at least the videos,
there are versions

00:08:49.530 --> 00:08:52.640
that have been either
dubbed or redone in Arabic.

00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:57.640
So, definitely try to see if
you can find those resources.

00:08:59.050 --> 00:09:00.847
Elliot H. from YouTube says,

00:09:00.847 --> 00:09:04.380
"Did you have any earlier
startups that failed?"

00:09:04.380 --> 00:09:06.350
Simple answer is yes.

00:09:06.350 --> 00:09:09.943
I have had my share of failures.

00:09:11.371 --> 00:09:12.770
I don't even remember them all.

00:09:12.770 --> 00:09:15.110
I'm someone that probably I've always had

00:09:15.110 --> 00:09:17.760
a few hair-brained schemes
as early as I remember.

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:22.760
I remember in college,
I was trying to start,

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:25.560
actually we won some
business plan competitions.

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:28.710
I was trying to start an effort...

00:09:28.710 --> 00:09:29.840
Looks at my internet connection

00:09:29.840 --> 00:09:31.440
is doing weird things right now.

00:09:33.864 --> 00:09:35.450
I hope you all can hear me

00:09:35.450 --> 00:09:39.550
but in college I started an effort that,

00:09:39.550 --> 00:09:41.210
it was actually around education,

00:09:41.210 --> 00:09:43.890
where if you have an EEG which can measure

00:09:43.890 --> 00:09:46.170
kind of mental activity,

00:09:46.170 --> 00:09:48.398
can measure the various
cycles of brainwaves,

00:09:48.398 --> 00:09:53.100
that if students are able to
stay focused on something,

00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:56.810
say doing math problems,
it would reward them

00:09:56.810 --> 00:09:58.900
and that if they lose
their focus while doing it,

00:09:58.900 --> 00:10:01.317
it would say, "Hey, come back, stop, stop.

00:10:01.317 --> 00:10:03.690
"Come look at what, stay
focused on the problem."

00:10:03.690 --> 00:10:06.810
So, we'd call it Cerebral Technologies

00:10:06.810 --> 00:10:08.840
but we never got too far with that.

00:10:08.840 --> 00:10:11.330
Then, during my first job on the side,

00:10:11.330 --> 00:10:15.440
I was trying to, I had
decided this was in the early,

00:10:15.440 --> 00:10:16.880
this was in the late 90s.

00:10:16.880 --> 00:10:19.383
I had this idea of locally
scoped domain names.

00:10:21.400 --> 00:10:24.610
There's obviously domain
names for the whole world

00:10:24.610 --> 00:10:26.660
but like, what about pizza.com?

00:10:26.660 --> 00:10:29.240
What, shouldn't that go to
your local pizza parlor,

00:10:29.240 --> 00:10:32.416
not just to some random
global pizza place?

00:10:32.416 --> 00:10:35.060
So that was the idea, that you
could get these domain names,

00:10:35.060 --> 00:10:36.550
would they go to different places

00:10:36.550 --> 00:10:38.190
depending on where you are?

00:10:38.190 --> 00:10:40.610
We got a little far but
that didn't go too far

00:10:40.610 --> 00:10:44.070
and then I joined a
startup in the late 90s

00:10:44.070 --> 00:10:46.130
called MiVC and this was
a legitimate startup.

00:10:46.130 --> 00:10:47.700
It had venture funding and all the rest

00:10:47.700 --> 00:10:49.500
and I was their kind of their CTO

00:10:49.500 --> 00:10:52.130
and it got pretty far,
we grew to 40 people.

00:10:52.130 --> 00:10:54.060
We actually, the whole
idea was to democratize

00:10:54.060 --> 00:10:57.910
venture capital and the reason
why they even needed a CTO

00:10:57.910 --> 00:10:59.300
is that we were building a platform

00:10:59.300 --> 00:11:02.310
so that anyone who invested even a dollar

00:11:02.310 --> 00:11:03.942
into the venture capitalist funds,

00:11:03.942 --> 00:11:06.930
in the venture capital
funds could see what,

00:11:06.930 --> 00:11:08.290
understand the entrepreneurs,

00:11:08.290 --> 00:11:10.660
understand the businesses
that they were investing in,

00:11:10.660 --> 00:11:11.493
things like that

00:11:13.020 --> 00:11:16.320
but then the NASDAQ
collapsed, what was it?

00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:19.157
In spring of 2000 and that's when I said,

00:11:19.157 --> 00:11:20.197
"Maybe I should go to business school

00:11:20.197 --> 00:11:21.243
"and then even..."

00:11:22.210 --> 00:11:25.640
And actually even before business school,

00:11:25.640 --> 00:11:28.170
me and a buddy tried to,

00:11:28.170 --> 00:11:30.140
we started this thing called Truth Bites,

00:11:30.140 --> 00:11:32.039
which in hindsight, we
probably should have kept that.

00:11:32.039 --> 00:11:35.080
It was pre-the big social media wave

00:11:35.080 --> 00:11:37.290
but it was essentially, you
sign up all your friends

00:11:37.290 --> 00:11:39.570
and you could ask
questions that you normally

00:11:40.590 --> 00:11:42.870
feel less comfortable being open about

00:11:42.870 --> 00:11:46.030
and you could answer them
with some level of anonymity

00:11:47.150 --> 00:11:49.900
and it was kind of a
way to foster more open

00:11:49.900 --> 00:11:52.190
and vulnerable
communication between folks.

00:11:52.190 --> 00:11:57.190
So that's just some of my
list of startups, (laughs)

00:11:57.240 --> 00:12:00.360
failed startups and actually there's more.

00:12:00.360 --> 00:12:02.730
There's actually, I joined a hedge fund

00:12:02.730 --> 00:12:06.410
out of business school and
it was called Wool Capital.

00:12:06.410 --> 00:12:07.630
It was just me and my boss, Dan

00:12:07.630 --> 00:12:09.940
and we ended up hiring a few more analysts

00:12:09.940 --> 00:12:13.330
and it did very well but
then Dan decided to retire

00:12:13.330 --> 00:12:15.930
and he encouraged me to start my own fund.

00:12:15.930 --> 00:12:20.570
And so I literally took over
his expenses and his team,

00:12:20.570 --> 00:12:22.400
his office, which was very expensive.

00:12:22.400 --> 00:12:23.640
It was costing a lot of money

00:12:23.640 --> 00:12:27.440
and I started something called
Conduct Capital management

00:12:27.440 --> 00:12:31.380
and that was a month before fall of 2008

00:12:31.380 --> 00:12:34.550
where essentially the entire
stock market collapsed.

00:12:34.550 --> 00:12:36.830
And I still I'm a little
defensive about it

00:12:36.830 --> 00:12:39.410
because Conduct Capital did exist.

00:12:39.410 --> 00:12:41.050
It existed for about eight months

00:12:41.050 --> 00:12:43.500
and even in that year,
we were up 4% in a year

00:12:43.500 --> 00:12:45.090
that the market was down 40%.

00:12:45.090 --> 00:12:48.740
So, that's my slight
insecurity trying to justify.

00:12:48.740 --> 00:12:51.360
It failed despite performance

00:12:51.360 --> 00:12:53.200
but it obviously wasn't an environment

00:12:53.200 --> 00:12:55.010
where people were interested in investing

00:12:55.010 --> 00:12:56.720
in a new investment fund

00:12:56.720 --> 00:12:59.530
and so that actually, that was a tough one

00:12:59.530 --> 00:13:00.580
'cause that actually,

00:13:01.600 --> 00:13:04.330
we burned a lot of our
savings frankly, on that.

00:13:04.330 --> 00:13:06.150
So that was actually
one of the harder ones.

00:13:06.150 --> 00:13:07.850
I can laugh about it in hindsight.

00:13:09.071 --> 00:13:11.430
And then Khan Academy, so yes.

00:13:11.430 --> 00:13:13.310
I think that's actually a
pretty comprehensive list

00:13:13.310 --> 00:13:14.313
but a good question.

00:13:15.910 --> 00:13:18.977
So Maya Sharma from YouTube
says, "Are we in a recession

00:13:18.977 --> 00:13:21.070
"and what stocks should we invest in?"

00:13:21.070 --> 00:13:22.770
So the first part is easy to answer.

00:13:22.770 --> 00:13:24.340
I think the simple answer is yes.

00:13:24.340 --> 00:13:26.540
In a recession there's multiple

00:13:26.540 --> 00:13:28.850
strict definitions of it but
it's usually two quarters

00:13:28.850 --> 00:13:32.310
of kind of reduced economic growth,

00:13:32.310 --> 00:13:35.060
obviously because of the quarantines

00:13:35.060 --> 00:13:37.210
and then even once we're,
the stay in place orders

00:13:37.210 --> 00:13:38.240
and then even once we're out of it,

00:13:38.240 --> 00:13:39.590
people's behavior is going to change.

00:13:39.590 --> 00:13:40.630
They're going to travel less,

00:13:40.630 --> 00:13:42.670
they're going to go to restaurants less,

00:13:42.670 --> 00:13:44.080
even the restaurants that are open,

00:13:44.080 --> 00:13:45.590
they're gonna have to have less capacity

00:13:45.590 --> 00:13:46.940
'cause they're gonna have
to be socially distanced.

00:13:46.940 --> 00:13:49.470
So you're definitely, and you just look

00:13:49.470 --> 00:13:50.470
at the unemployment numbers,

00:13:50.470 --> 00:13:52.030
not just in the US, around the world.

00:13:52.030 --> 00:13:53.060
And so it's very clear

00:13:53.060 --> 00:13:55.280
we're for sure in a recession already

00:13:56.170 --> 00:13:58.220
and unfortunately the question is,

00:13:58.220 --> 00:14:00.283
how bad will it be?

00:14:02.294 --> 00:14:06.150
I'm not in the investment world anymore.

00:14:06.150 --> 00:14:08.310
We've had some interesting
folks like Ray Daleo

00:14:08.310 --> 00:14:11.300
and Mellody Hobson who are
some of the top investors

00:14:11.300 --> 00:14:13.440
in the world on this live stream

00:14:13.440 --> 00:14:16.130
and from what I hear from them,

00:14:16.130 --> 00:14:17.830
there's definitely...

00:14:19.190 --> 00:14:22.480
The stock market's incredibly
volatile right now.

00:14:22.480 --> 00:14:24.730
That's very hard to time when you get in

00:14:24.730 --> 00:14:26.730
but when there is a lot of volatility

00:14:26.730 --> 00:14:28.560
and a lot of emotions in the market,

00:14:28.560 --> 00:14:31.280
that is a time that
interesting opportunities

00:14:31.280 --> 00:14:32.930
can sometimes get lost in the fray.

00:14:32.930 --> 00:14:36.090
So I would say if you are
diligent and you look for things

00:14:36.090 --> 00:14:37.700
and you look for things
that have a good thesis

00:14:37.700 --> 00:14:41.440
and a thesis that can do
well in any type of economy,

00:14:41.440 --> 00:14:46.050
you might find some diamonds
in the rough, so to speak.

00:14:46.050 --> 00:14:48.863
But I do say that,

00:14:50.160 --> 00:14:53.010
I know how sophisticated
many investors are

00:14:53.010 --> 00:14:54.480
and I know all of the
work that we used to do

00:14:54.480 --> 00:14:56.460
when I was an analyst at a hedge fund

00:14:56.460 --> 00:14:59.980
and so I'm actually afraid
to be a stock picker today

00:14:59.980 --> 00:15:02.770
because I know that
there's a version of myself

00:15:02.770 --> 00:15:04.370
on the other side of that trade

00:15:04.370 --> 00:15:07.050
who's doing a lot more
analysis than I have the time

00:15:07.050 --> 00:15:08.500
to do right now.

00:15:08.500 --> 00:15:09.760
So most of most...

00:15:09.760 --> 00:15:11.333
I do it for 401K.

00:15:13.240 --> 00:15:15.970
That's passively, it's
just an index things

00:15:15.970 --> 00:15:18.450
but most of my savings,

00:15:18.450 --> 00:15:20.280
I use it to pay down our mortgage

00:15:20.280 --> 00:15:24.900
and to, we do have like a
rental property (laughs)

00:15:24.900 --> 00:15:26.700
and I don't know, I have no idea

00:15:26.700 --> 00:15:28.300
how that's all going to play out

00:15:29.884 --> 00:15:31.050
with what's going on.

00:15:31.050 --> 00:15:33.520
It's gonna be a tough, my best prediction,

00:15:33.520 --> 00:15:37.780
it's gonna be a tough
couple of years coming up.

00:15:37.780 --> 00:15:39.970
So yeah, I definitely would shy away

00:15:39.970 --> 00:15:41.510
from doing anything very risky

00:15:41.510 --> 00:15:42.680
but this is not a place for me

00:15:42.680 --> 00:15:44.050
to give you too much investment advice,

00:15:44.050 --> 00:15:45.700
take it all with a grain of salt.

00:15:47.588 --> 00:15:52.500
So, Zaria Khan from YouTube,
not a question just a comment

00:15:52.500 --> 00:15:56.340
says, "I remember seeing
him younger than this,"

00:15:56.340 --> 00:15:58.790
So yes, I guess I've aged.

00:15:58.790 --> 00:16:02.320
Thank you for pointing that out. (laughs)

00:16:02.320 --> 00:16:06.980
In fairness, many years of you
might remember Khan Academy,

00:16:06.980 --> 00:16:09.530
Sal Khan from 2008, 2009.

00:16:09.530 --> 00:16:10.800
That's when we first
started really getting

00:16:10.800 --> 00:16:13.650
on people's radars and
it's been 11 years since

00:16:13.650 --> 00:16:18.260
and so I try to wear the cream
and the lotion and sunscreen

00:16:18.260 --> 00:16:21.920
but we all age (laughs)

00:16:21.920 --> 00:16:25.500
and the beard I heard, 'cause
it's gray, it has gray in it.

00:16:25.500 --> 00:16:29.073
So whatever, it's a trade off.

00:16:30.050 --> 00:16:32.870
These are good questions and comments.

00:16:32.870 --> 00:16:35.527
From YouTube, Michelle Mitchell says,

00:16:35.527 --> 00:16:38.880
"Hi, Sal, how did you
pick a major in college?"

00:16:38.880 --> 00:16:39.830
Good question, Michelle.

00:16:39.830 --> 00:16:44.150
So, for me, well, I'll tell
you what my thinking was

00:16:44.150 --> 00:16:45.950
when I was maybe your age

00:16:45.950 --> 00:16:47.950
if you are picking that major right now.

00:16:49.511 --> 00:16:51.820
I guess you could say I was a romantic

00:16:51.820 --> 00:16:55.430
in that when I was in high
school, I was convinced,

00:16:55.430 --> 00:16:57.280
I was obsessed with physics.

00:16:57.280 --> 00:16:59.580
I said, I wanna be a theoretical physics,

00:16:59.580 --> 00:17:03.360
a PhD one day, I wanna
ponder the nature of reality.

00:17:03.360 --> 00:17:05.902
I wanna answer the big questions

00:17:05.902 --> 00:17:08.260
and even in my college applications,

00:17:08.260 --> 00:17:09.870
when they said, what
do you wanna major in?

00:17:09.870 --> 00:17:11.770
I said, "Physics, theoretical physics,"

00:17:11.770 --> 00:17:14.120
and when I got to college,

00:17:14.120 --> 00:17:16.200
it was actually around that
same summer before college,

00:17:16.200 --> 00:17:18.790
there was a local professor

00:17:18.790 --> 00:17:21.870
that found out that I had
a lot of interest in math

00:17:21.870 --> 00:17:23.700
and computing and also found out

00:17:23.700 --> 00:17:25.660
that I didn't have a computer.

00:17:25.660 --> 00:17:27.390
And so he had access to computers.

00:17:27.390 --> 00:17:29.570
So he gave me a summer internship

00:17:29.570 --> 00:17:32.210
and so I was able to
essentially learn to code

00:17:32.210 --> 00:17:35.950
using a proper computer,
actually quite good computers.

00:17:35.950 --> 00:17:38.248
These kinds of mini computers
that are one step up

00:17:38.248 --> 00:17:41.830
above PC type, back then in the mid 90s

00:17:41.830 --> 00:17:44.560
and that's where I started
getting a real love

00:17:44.560 --> 00:17:46.531
for computing and I said,
"Well, there's something

00:17:46.531 --> 00:17:51.531
"about programming where
you can create realities,"

00:17:51.660 --> 00:17:53.780
and people even debate
whether the reality we live in

00:17:53.780 --> 00:17:58.460
is a simulation but that
kinda got me excited

00:17:58.460 --> 00:17:59.870
about, hey, could you create realities?

00:17:59.870 --> 00:18:00.980
Could you create experiences?

00:18:00.980 --> 00:18:02.740
And there was also something about

00:18:02.740 --> 00:18:04.410
technology and programming
that I liked is that

00:18:04.410 --> 00:18:05.710
it could scale.

00:18:05.710 --> 00:18:08.950
If you create an interesting
experience in theory,

00:18:08.950 --> 00:18:11.350
an unlimited number of people could use it

00:18:11.350 --> 00:18:12.670
and that was pretty compelling.

00:18:12.670 --> 00:18:16.350
And so I decided to become
a computer science major

00:18:16.350 --> 00:18:18.240
and math major at the same time

00:18:18.240 --> 00:18:20.430
and that's the path I went down

00:18:20.430 --> 00:18:24.220
and as I got further in my undergrad,

00:18:24.220 --> 00:18:26.310
I started thinking about,
well, what about money?

00:18:26.310 --> 00:18:28.380
And I didn't come from a wealthy family.

00:18:28.380 --> 00:18:32.220
My mother made, I think, $16,000 in 1993

00:18:32.220 --> 00:18:33.450
and the only reason why I remember that

00:18:33.450 --> 00:18:36.200
is I had to do her taxes so
that I could get financial aid.

00:18:36.200 --> 00:18:38.050
So I remember the number very clearly

00:18:40.870 --> 00:18:42.950
and so, I was...

00:18:42.950 --> 00:18:45.280
MIT was very generous
with the financial aid

00:18:45.280 --> 00:18:48.070
but I still was incurring about,

00:18:48.070 --> 00:18:50.900
give or take about $10,000 a year in debt

00:18:50.900 --> 00:18:53.340
and that was a lot of money for my family.

00:18:53.340 --> 00:18:56.070
And so near the end of my undergrad,

00:18:56.070 --> 00:18:57.650
I started thinking very seriously about,

00:18:57.650 --> 00:18:58.660
how do I pay this back?

00:18:58.660 --> 00:18:59.600
How do I start making money?

00:18:59.600 --> 00:19:01.180
How do I help out my family?

00:19:01.180 --> 00:19:03.130
And I was going to do a master's

00:19:03.130 --> 00:19:06.154
but I kind of hurried that up
by taking a few more classes

00:19:06.154 --> 00:19:08.750
and the good thing is it was the late 90s

00:19:08.750 --> 00:19:10.960
and I hadn't really thought about it much

00:19:10.960 --> 00:19:14.930
but kind of CS was a very,

00:19:14.930 --> 00:19:18.270
it was a very in demand
career as it continues to be.

00:19:18.270 --> 00:19:19.590
So it worked out financially.

00:19:19.590 --> 00:19:20.423
Actually I'll take that back.

00:19:20.423 --> 00:19:22.060
I sound like I didn't
think about it at all.

00:19:22.060 --> 00:19:26.210
I think, when you come from
fairly humble circumstances,

00:19:26.210 --> 00:19:28.810
I don't think you oftentimes
even allow yourself

00:19:28.810 --> 00:19:31.640
to go into a field that might be viewed

00:19:31.640 --> 00:19:33.100
as a little less than practical.

00:19:33.100 --> 00:19:34.130
So I think that was definitely

00:19:34.130 --> 00:19:35.630
one of the constraints for me.

00:19:36.824 --> 00:19:40.030
If I were giving advice to
you or to a family member

00:19:40.030 --> 00:19:42.970
who's 17, 18 years old, 19 years old

00:19:42.970 --> 00:19:44.470
and thinking about their major,

00:19:44.470 --> 00:19:45.683
I would say,

00:19:49.300 --> 00:19:50.290
especially in the economy

00:19:50.290 --> 00:19:52.610
that we're about to go
in, look at the fields

00:19:52.610 --> 00:19:54.803
that there's demand for.

00:19:56.690 --> 00:19:58.550
I know this sounds like
very pragmatic advice

00:19:58.550 --> 00:20:00.140
'cause it is but

00:20:02.489 --> 00:20:05.560
if you're able to kinda
put money on the table,

00:20:05.560 --> 00:20:07.310
food on the table, however
you wanna describe it

00:20:07.310 --> 00:20:10.660
and you feel productive,
that is going to open up

00:20:10.660 --> 00:20:13.270
a lot more things to you in your life.

00:20:13.270 --> 00:20:16.800
And so, everyone talks about computing now

00:20:16.800 --> 00:20:18.800
and obviously if that's
something you're excited about,

00:20:18.800 --> 00:20:20.120
definitely go into it

00:20:20.120 --> 00:20:22.593
but there's a lot of
other interesting areas.

00:20:24.230 --> 00:20:25.870
Growing up, I thought
something like accounting

00:20:25.870 --> 00:20:27.960
is kind of, that's been
counting, et cetera, et cetera

00:20:27.960 --> 00:20:30.170
but then when I went to business school,

00:20:30.170 --> 00:20:31.790
I discovered that finance and accounting

00:20:31.790 --> 00:20:33.090
are fascinating subjects

00:20:33.090 --> 00:20:35.510
and that's why actually why
I had this career transition

00:20:35.510 --> 00:20:36.343
into investing.

00:20:36.343 --> 00:20:38.170
So I think there's a lot of things

00:20:38.170 --> 00:20:40.820
that we very easily can get
programmed by stereotypes.

00:20:40.820 --> 00:20:42.257
You might have a friend
or family member who said,

00:20:42.257 --> 00:20:43.627
"Oh, no, don't go into that.

00:20:43.627 --> 00:20:45.230
"Those people are like this or like that,"

00:20:45.230 --> 00:20:47.930
or, "That career is
boring," but give it a shot.

00:20:47.930 --> 00:20:50.070
And almost any field,

00:20:50.070 --> 00:20:51.730
if you put yourself in the right mindset

00:20:51.730 --> 00:20:53.980
actually can be fascinating.

00:20:53.980 --> 00:20:56.850
So that's the best advice I could give

00:20:56.850 --> 00:21:00.380
but definitely try to come out
of college with some skills

00:21:00.380 --> 00:21:03.220
that you think have a good chance of,

00:21:03.220 --> 00:21:04.610
you're gonna be able to pay off your debt

00:21:04.610 --> 00:21:05.443
and things like that.

00:21:05.443 --> 00:21:07.110
I do have people in my family

00:21:07.110 --> 00:21:10.690
and this is just a microcosm
for the whole country.

00:21:10.690 --> 00:21:13.390
I don't think colleges
talk to freshmen enough

00:21:13.390 --> 00:21:15.270
about the implications of the debt

00:21:15.270 --> 00:21:17.780
and that how it can stay
with you a very long time

00:21:17.780 --> 00:21:19.970
and I don't think they give
enough data to freshmen

00:21:19.970 --> 00:21:22.200
and sophomores who are picking
their majors to tell them,

00:21:22.200 --> 00:21:24.250
okay, people who majored in X,

00:21:24.250 --> 00:21:27.440
what are their job prospects
like when they graduate?

00:21:27.440 --> 00:21:28.273
How much are they making?

00:21:28.273 --> 00:21:30.150
What percentage of them
have paid off their debt?

00:21:30.150 --> 00:21:32.450
What percent of them are
in default of their debt,

00:21:32.450 --> 00:21:33.990
10 years out, 15 years out?

00:21:33.990 --> 00:21:37.110
And I know these are
kinda buzzkill answers

00:21:37.110 --> 00:21:38.780
but I think it's super important.

00:21:38.780 --> 00:21:41.790
So, if you can try to do some
of that research yourself,

00:21:41.790 --> 00:21:43.410
talk to people who go to your college

00:21:43.410 --> 00:21:45.380
and say, try to find alumns

00:21:45.380 --> 00:21:48.060
who are in the major you're interested in,

00:21:48.060 --> 00:21:50.330
they're one year out, five
year out, 10 year out,

00:21:50.330 --> 00:21:52.630
10 years out and I think
there'll be very frank with you.

00:21:52.630 --> 00:21:53.957
Some of them will say,
"It was the best decision

00:21:53.957 --> 00:21:55.430
"I ever made to major in X,"

00:21:55.430 --> 00:21:59.727
and some will say, "Ah, it's
not as easy as I thought

00:21:59.727 --> 00:22:01.070
"to make a living in this X,"

00:22:01.070 --> 00:22:02.430
or maybe I am making a living

00:22:02.430 --> 00:22:04.630
but the subject matter is one thing

00:22:04.630 --> 00:22:06.110
but then the job is a different thing.

00:22:06.110 --> 00:22:09.310
So that's the best advice I could give.

00:22:09.310 --> 00:22:10.530
I will give a plug.

00:22:10.530 --> 00:22:12.950
Bank of America has been a
long time sponsor of work

00:22:12.950 --> 00:22:16.230
we've been doing in financial
literacy and in careers

00:22:16.230 --> 00:22:18.280
and so look up Khan Academy careers

00:22:18.280 --> 00:22:20.160
and better money habits at Bank of America

00:22:20.160 --> 00:22:22.030
and you'll actually see
a bunch of video profiles

00:22:22.030 --> 00:22:23.900
of people in different careers

00:22:23.900 --> 00:22:26.463
and understanding the trade offs in them.

00:22:27.320 --> 00:22:32.320
Okay, so Abijith Paccar
says, "What inspires you

00:22:32.747 --> 00:22:35.060
"to learn various topics?"

00:22:35.060 --> 00:22:40.020
Well, for me, we're in
this mystery called life

00:22:40.020 --> 00:22:43.393
and I've said it before
on this live stream,

00:22:45.242 --> 00:22:46.560
there's been thousands of years

00:22:46.560 --> 00:22:49.070
of accumulated human knowledge

00:22:49.070 --> 00:22:52.210
and most of the things
that are now a paragraph

00:22:52.210 --> 00:22:54.900
in your textbook or a
video on Khan Academy,

00:22:54.900 --> 00:22:56.470
that's oftentimes the culmination

00:22:56.470 --> 00:23:00.290
of many people's lifetime work
and they would do anything,

00:23:00.290 --> 00:23:01.480
they would have done anything

00:23:01.480 --> 00:23:03.100
to have access to that information

00:23:03.100 --> 00:23:05.230
and so I always remind myself,

00:23:05.230 --> 00:23:06.960
there's something interesting about this,

00:23:06.960 --> 00:23:09.300
that someone wanted to
devote their life to

00:23:09.300 --> 00:23:10.793
and there usually is.

00:23:12.110 --> 00:23:13.773
The science, any subject.

00:23:15.286 --> 00:23:16.560
When you look at literature

00:23:16.560 --> 00:23:21.560
and how it can evoke human emotions

00:23:22.333 --> 00:23:25.340
and transport you to another time

00:23:25.340 --> 00:23:28.640
or another even perspective,
that's somewhat magical.

00:23:28.640 --> 00:23:29.770
If you look at the sciences,

00:23:29.770 --> 00:23:31.482
they really are all about understanding

00:23:31.482 --> 00:23:34.980
the nature of our reality,
the nature of life,

00:23:34.980 --> 00:23:36.123
the nature of how all,

00:23:38.620 --> 00:23:40.930
just a handful of elements can combine

00:23:40.930 --> 00:23:45.430
in different ways to
form a Sal or an Abijith.

00:23:45.430 --> 00:23:47.760
These are mind blowing things

00:23:47.760 --> 00:23:50.270
so that's what gets me excited

00:23:50.270 --> 00:23:52.440
and obviously I have an extra motivation

00:23:52.440 --> 00:23:54.080
that if I can really digest it

00:23:54.080 --> 00:23:56.880
and stay excited about
it, then I can hopefully

00:23:56.880 --> 00:23:58.320
be a little bit infectious with that

00:23:58.320 --> 00:24:01.233
and help other people get
excited about it as well.

00:24:02.403 --> 00:24:07.217
So, from Joanna Mina, from Facebook,

00:24:07.217 --> 00:24:08.847
"Sal, do you have
suggestions how to keep kids

00:24:08.847 --> 00:24:10.457
"engaged in online school?

00:24:10.457 --> 00:24:12.337
"Sometimes they study to have
good grades for their teachers

00:24:12.337 --> 00:24:13.177
"but while studying from home,

00:24:13.177 --> 00:24:14.707
"they don't have the
engagement with other humans

00:24:14.707 --> 00:24:16.420
"and lose interest."

00:24:16.420 --> 00:24:19.500
You're asking a super
important question, Joanne

00:24:21.860 --> 00:24:25.350
and I won't pretend like I've
solved all of it at home.

00:24:25.350 --> 00:24:27.683
My older children, they go to school

00:24:27.683 --> 00:24:30.280
that I helped start called Khan Lab school

00:24:30.280 --> 00:24:32.930
and they've done a really good job

00:24:32.930 --> 00:24:36.020
of essentially helping young people.

00:24:36.020 --> 00:24:39.140
My kids are 11, eight and five

00:24:39.140 --> 00:24:40.730
and especially the 11 and eight year old,

00:24:40.730 --> 00:24:42.380
who've been in kind of in the system

00:24:42.380 --> 00:24:45.680
where there's been a lot
of emphasis on agency.

00:24:45.680 --> 00:24:47.850
Students set their goals
but then once a week,

00:24:47.850 --> 00:24:50.770
they check in with their
teachers to review their goal.

00:24:50.770 --> 00:24:52.730
So there is accountability around it

00:24:52.730 --> 00:24:55.810
but that muscle that they've
been building over many years,

00:24:55.810 --> 00:24:59.420
even pre-COVID, it seems to
be serving them well right now

00:24:59.420 --> 00:25:02.020
and their teachers are
doing an incredible job,

00:25:02.020 --> 00:25:04.590
kind of keeping it going over the course

00:25:04.590 --> 00:25:06.794
of this situation we're in.

00:25:06.794 --> 00:25:09.790
So I do see even young kids
as young as seven, eight,

00:25:09.790 --> 00:25:11.030
nine years old,

00:25:11.030 --> 00:25:12.620
if they're used to that muscle of,

00:25:12.620 --> 00:25:15.330
okay, you have a set number
of goals for the day,

00:25:15.330 --> 00:25:18.950
for the week, for the term
and that there's a daily

00:25:18.950 --> 00:25:22.703
and then maybe weekly or maybe
quarterly or term check-in,

00:25:22.703 --> 00:25:24.460
that actually can be pretty good

00:25:24.460 --> 00:25:26.100
and then on the day to day,

00:25:26.100 --> 00:25:27.330
I think it's all about structure.

00:25:27.330 --> 00:25:28.440
It's all about habit.

00:25:28.440 --> 00:25:30.400
Our five-year-old has been
maybe the most difficult

00:25:30.400 --> 00:25:33.260
but even with him, the
more we get the structure

00:25:33.260 --> 00:25:36.280
and the habit and we
celebrate the small wins

00:25:36.280 --> 00:25:38.450
and we make sure there's
a lot of time for breaks

00:25:38.450 --> 00:25:40.970
and we don't try to do too much at once.

00:25:40.970 --> 00:25:42.920
We say, "Hey," for the five-year-old.

00:25:42.920 --> 00:25:45.810
If we can get a productive
hour and a half today,

00:25:45.810 --> 00:25:46.643
that's a win.

00:25:46.643 --> 00:25:47.850
And then once we get into the habit

00:25:47.850 --> 00:25:49.020
of that productive hour and a half,

00:25:49.020 --> 00:25:52.610
we're going to keep going
but then for my older kids,

00:25:52.610 --> 00:25:54.640
it's all about just making
sure they have balance,

00:25:54.640 --> 00:25:57.141
even in these times of social distancing,

00:25:57.141 --> 00:25:59.500
that they have time to go outside,

00:25:59.500 --> 00:26:01.410
that they have time to play.

00:26:01.410 --> 00:26:03.070
They have time to do creative things

00:26:03.070 --> 00:26:04.970
that while they're doing
their independent work

00:26:04.970 --> 00:26:07.819
or their zoom sessions
with their teachers,

00:26:07.819 --> 00:26:09.810
that they're taking breaks,

00:26:09.810 --> 00:26:11.370
they're stretching and things like that.

00:26:11.370 --> 00:26:13.680
That's the best I can give,
there's other resources.

00:26:13.680 --> 00:26:15.250
We've had parents and teacher webinars

00:26:15.250 --> 00:26:17.000
that go into much more depth on this

00:26:17.000 --> 00:26:18.300
and obviously we've released things

00:26:18.300 --> 00:26:22.190
like daily schedules and learning plans

00:26:22.190 --> 00:26:23.540
to help with some of this

00:26:23.540 --> 00:26:27.440
but I think those check ins
with both parents and teachers

00:26:27.440 --> 00:26:29.250
that hold the students
a little bit accountable

00:26:29.250 --> 00:26:30.140
and they know they're gonna come,

00:26:30.140 --> 00:26:31.380
that helps them build that muscle.

00:26:31.380 --> 00:26:32.940
So I would just say stick to it.

00:26:32.940 --> 00:26:34.790
It can sometimes be a
little messy at first

00:26:34.790 --> 00:26:36.700
but over time, if there's a silver lining,

00:26:36.700 --> 00:26:38.730
is that students could
build more of that agency

00:26:38.730 --> 00:26:40.180
and that independence muscle.

00:26:41.230 --> 00:26:43.163
So let's see.

00:26:44.165 --> 00:26:49.165
From, Afthab Khan from
Facebook, "Any plans in India?"

00:26:50.760 --> 00:26:52.830
We actually have a whole team in India,

00:26:52.830 --> 00:26:54.370
Khan Academy, India team.

00:26:54.370 --> 00:26:57.870
There's several folks in
Delhi and in Bangalore.

00:26:57.870 --> 00:26:59.680
So yeah, simple answer's is we have plans.

00:26:59.680 --> 00:27:01.920
I could go over the whole Indie effort

00:27:01.920 --> 00:27:03.820
but if you do some web searches on it,

00:27:03.820 --> 00:27:05.920
you should be able to find our India...

00:27:05.920 --> 00:27:08.340
We have a Hi-English
version of Khan Academy,

00:27:08.340 --> 00:27:10.350
which is for those who
don't know Hi-English,

00:27:10.350 --> 00:27:13.320
it's a hybrid between Hindi and English.

00:27:13.320 --> 00:27:15.810
We're translating into,
on redoing actually

00:27:15.810 --> 00:27:17.950
in many of the languages in India,

00:27:17.950 --> 00:27:19.800
we're mapping to many
of the state standards.

00:27:19.800 --> 00:27:22.470
So India is actually
one of our geographies

00:27:22.470 --> 00:27:23.450
that we're very focused on.

00:27:23.450 --> 00:27:26.460
The other is Brazil, Spanish
speaking Latin America

00:27:26.460 --> 00:27:27.800
and that has, as I mentioned,

00:27:27.800 --> 00:27:29.540
there's 45 translation projects

00:27:29.540 --> 00:27:32.143
from mainly volunteers around the world.

00:27:33.210 --> 00:27:38.210
So Adam Bupkari asks, "Is
it possible to take the SAT

00:27:39.030 --> 00:27:40.450
from Khan Academy?"

00:27:40.450 --> 00:27:42.590
You could prepare for
the SAT from Khan Academy

00:27:42.590 --> 00:27:46.870
but the administration
is not on Khan Academy

00:27:46.870 --> 00:27:48.420
but I highly recommend,

00:27:48.420 --> 00:27:51.403
even if you're not
taking the SAT right now,

00:27:52.261 --> 00:27:54.260
when we partnered with
the Cardboard on this,

00:27:54.260 --> 00:27:55.130
we said, we don't want this

00:27:55.130 --> 00:27:56.950
just to be superficial test prep,

00:27:56.950 --> 00:27:59.110
we want this to be something
that really helps students

00:27:59.110 --> 00:28:00.580
become more college ready,

00:28:00.580 --> 00:28:03.190
improves their mathematical
critical thinking,

00:28:03.190 --> 00:28:06.130
their reading comprehension,
their writing skills.

00:28:06.130 --> 00:28:07.820
So actually our SAT practice,

00:28:07.820 --> 00:28:09.730
the official SAT practice on Khan Academy,

00:28:09.730 --> 00:28:12.570
I actually think it's a great
thing for anyone for sure.

00:28:12.570 --> 00:28:13.970
Any high school students and even maybe

00:28:13.970 --> 00:28:16.450
some middle school
students to start doing,

00:28:16.450 --> 00:28:19.030
putting in half an hour
a day, 40 minutes a day.

00:28:19.030 --> 00:28:20.810
If you do that, I think you're gonna find

00:28:20.810 --> 00:28:22.900
that you're gonna really
build those muscles,

00:28:22.900 --> 00:28:25.750
especially through this
kind of COVID crisis

00:28:25.750 --> 00:28:27.520
and through the summer
and then when you happen

00:28:27.520 --> 00:28:28.827
to take the SAT, you're
just gonna be like,

00:28:28.827 --> 00:28:30.720
"Oh, this all makes sense."

00:28:30.720 --> 00:28:32.840
This is all pretty straightforward

00:28:32.840 --> 00:28:35.690
and frankly, you'll just be
that much more college ready.

00:28:38.355 --> 00:28:40.890
So, from YouTube explorer questioner,

00:28:40.890 --> 00:28:42.927
unorthodox question for Sal,

00:28:42.927 --> 00:28:45.267
"Do you perhaps garden?"

00:28:46.958 --> 00:28:49.160
A little bit, my wife is the gardener

00:28:49.160 --> 00:28:51.620
and she would like me
to be more of a gardener

00:28:51.620 --> 00:28:55.400
and, so I'm trying to garden more.

00:28:55.400 --> 00:28:56.690
My wife does a wonderful job.

00:28:56.690 --> 00:28:57.940
We have a very pleasant garden,

00:28:57.940 --> 00:28:59.800
maybe one day I'll do our livestream

00:28:59.800 --> 00:29:02.300
from our backyard

00:29:02.300 --> 00:29:05.240
but I definitely see the appeal

00:29:05.240 --> 00:29:06.470
but I just haven't...

00:29:07.530 --> 00:29:09.680
If I claim to do any gardening,

00:29:09.680 --> 00:29:11.500
if my wife is watching,
she would roll her eyes.

00:29:11.500 --> 00:29:14.543
So I won't make claims
that I can't back up.

00:29:15.450 --> 00:29:19.390
From YouTube, Anglo Indian
boy, "Do you play chess?"

00:29:19.390 --> 00:29:22.850
I do but I play at a,

00:29:22.850 --> 00:29:26.490
I would say, I'm a very good amateur

00:29:26.490 --> 00:29:31.400
and my 11 year old son is getting,

00:29:31.400 --> 00:29:32.570
we're about even now.

00:29:32.570 --> 00:29:35.460
So he's on the site, Chess Kids,

00:29:35.460 --> 00:29:37.393
which I have to give credit, it's,

00:29:38.950 --> 00:29:40.630
he spends time on that every...

00:29:40.630 --> 00:29:43.570
Whenever I win, he goes
and learns new things,

00:29:43.570 --> 00:29:45.480
new openings and new strategies

00:29:45.480 --> 00:29:47.863
and then he comes back and
then he usually beats me.

00:29:48.730 --> 00:29:50.880
He's on definitely a
much faster trajectory

00:29:50.880 --> 00:29:52.560
and I think we're about a year away

00:29:52.560 --> 00:29:55.400
where he might,

00:29:55.400 --> 00:29:57.320
I might not be able to beat him anymore

00:29:57.320 --> 00:29:59.020
but yeah, I would consider myself

00:29:59.020 --> 00:30:01.710
a good amateur chess player

00:30:01.710 --> 00:30:05.180
but I definitely love the game.

00:30:05.180 --> 00:30:06.013
Let's see.

00:30:06.970 --> 00:30:08.960
Well I do say, I mean, I
will brag a little bit,

00:30:08.960 --> 00:30:13.960
there was at this event in Silicon Valley,

00:30:14.350 --> 00:30:16.900
Magnus Carlsen was there, a chess champion

00:30:16.900 --> 00:30:19.080
for those of you who
don't know of the world.

00:30:19.080 --> 00:30:22.400
And he offered to play
four people blindfolded,

00:30:22.400 --> 00:30:24.690
and my name was picked from
the hat of the people there.

00:30:24.690 --> 00:30:25.800
So I was one of the four people

00:30:25.800 --> 00:30:27.710
who got to play Magnus Carlsen.

00:30:27.710 --> 00:30:31.080
He was blindfolded and playing
four of us at the same time

00:30:31.080 --> 00:30:33.070
but I was the last person standing.

00:30:33.070 --> 00:30:35.990
So there was, he took
care of the other three

00:30:35.990 --> 00:30:38.666
and then he's obviously blindfolded

00:30:38.666 --> 00:30:41.300
and there was a moment in the game,

00:30:41.300 --> 00:30:43.150
where I actually had an advantage,

00:30:43.150 --> 00:30:45.660
but then when his entire

00:30:45.660 --> 00:30:48.370
chess brain power was focused on me,

00:30:48.370 --> 00:30:49.600
I could almost like feel it

00:30:49.600 --> 00:30:51.830
and it was incredibly intimidating.

00:30:51.830 --> 00:30:55.080
And then I just panicked and he won,

00:30:55.080 --> 00:30:58.143
not to say that he wouldn't
have won if I didn't panic,

00:30:58.143 --> 00:31:00.226
(laughs)

00:31:01.289 --> 00:31:02.610
Favorite board game.

00:31:02.610 --> 00:31:03.443
We're all out of time

00:31:03.443 --> 00:31:04.276
but I can go a little bit longer.

00:31:04.276 --> 00:31:06.610
I think I have time.

00:31:06.610 --> 00:31:07.740
What's your favorite board game,

00:31:07.740 --> 00:31:11.220
from Alexander Ticevich,
sorry if I'm mispronouncing

00:31:11.220 --> 00:31:13.113
your name Alexander from Facebook.

00:31:14.840 --> 00:31:19.840
I'm a big fan of Risk,
Monopoly and Settlers of Catan

00:31:21.690 --> 00:31:24.410
but with all of them, they get much better

00:31:24.410 --> 00:31:28.850
if you add kind of a
notion of markets to them.

00:31:28.850 --> 00:31:31.300
So I've have a version
of Risk that I play.

00:31:31.300 --> 00:31:34.350
It's kind of a paranoia Risk where,

00:31:34.350 --> 00:31:37.400
paranoia Risk is where every army is,

00:31:37.400 --> 00:31:41.880
their goal is to try to remove
another army from the board

00:31:41.880 --> 00:31:43.880
but you don't know who's
trying to remove you

00:31:43.880 --> 00:31:46.354
and so you could imagine
there's a lot of intrigue

00:31:46.354 --> 00:31:49.710
and what you do is you can
create stocks for each color,

00:31:49.710 --> 00:31:51.610
and then you can have
another separate group

00:31:51.610 --> 00:31:54.250
of 20 to 30 people, trading securities.

00:31:54.250 --> 00:31:56.080
So if that color wins,
it's worth a hundred

00:31:56.080 --> 00:31:58.230
at the end of the game, if
they lose it is worth zero.

00:31:58.230 --> 00:31:59.740
And so at any given point in time,

00:31:59.740 --> 00:32:02.460
the value of that color trading
should be the probability

00:32:02.460 --> 00:32:03.730
that they win the game.

00:32:03.730 --> 00:32:04.870
That's a fun thing.

00:32:04.870 --> 00:32:09.640
In Monopoly, I like to
add notions of investors.

00:32:09.640 --> 00:32:11.990
So there's even people who are,

00:32:11.990 --> 00:32:13.358
they're willing to take equity stakes

00:32:13.358 --> 00:32:15.570
in various players,

00:32:15.570 --> 00:32:18.260
you can introduce things
like insurance contracts,

00:32:18.260 --> 00:32:20.930
debt instruments in monopoly.

00:32:20.930 --> 00:32:23.430
So I think that's pretty,
corporate structures

00:32:23.430 --> 00:32:26.204
so that you can have multiple
investors in like the shoe.

00:32:26.204 --> 00:32:27.740
(giggling)

00:32:27.740 --> 00:32:30.750
So that's a fun way to,
and in Settlers of Catan,

00:32:30.750 --> 00:32:32.470
you can actually, there's
something I used to do

00:32:32.470 --> 00:32:36.020
in summer camps I used to run
with middle school students.

00:32:36.020 --> 00:32:39.580
You can play Settlers of
Catan with no trading,

00:32:39.580 --> 00:32:41.520
you could play trading at your board.

00:32:41.520 --> 00:32:44.520
Then you can play trading
where you have other boards,

00:32:44.520 --> 00:32:45.353
and then you could trade.

00:32:45.353 --> 00:32:47.110
You can kind of do that
as international trade

00:32:47.110 --> 00:32:49.760
and then you could introduce
some form of currency

00:32:49.760 --> 00:32:51.880
and then what's interesting
is you can see it

00:32:51.880 --> 00:32:53.940
on every one of those steps,

00:32:53.940 --> 00:32:56.690
more trade, will create more building

00:32:56.690 --> 00:32:58.490
and more currency will
create more building

00:32:58.490 --> 00:33:00.210
just 'cause there's, more people are able

00:33:00.210 --> 00:33:01.150
to get what they need.

00:33:01.150 --> 00:33:03.300
So it's a good way of understanding money

00:33:03.300 --> 00:33:05.113
and the value of trade.

