00:35:18.447 --> 00:35:20.457
"Sal, where in the U.S would
be a good place to live

00:35:20.457 --> 00:35:22.337
"for a lifelong learner?"

00:35:23.270 --> 00:35:25.690
I've actually had this,
I've gone through this

00:35:25.690 --> 00:35:27.160
thought experiment before in my life,

00:35:27.160 --> 00:35:28.320
'cause as you can imagine,

00:35:28.320 --> 00:35:30.420
Silicon Valley is not
a cheap place to live

00:35:30.420 --> 00:35:33.760
and when Khan Academy was
just me in a walk in closet,

00:35:33.760 --> 00:35:35.010
and we were living off of savings,

00:35:35.010 --> 00:35:37.920
I used to look at other
places in the country

00:35:37.920 --> 00:35:38.930
that had a nice climate,

00:35:38.930 --> 00:35:41.110
that had a better cost of living

00:35:41.110 --> 00:35:42.980
and thought about whether
I should move there or not

00:35:42.980 --> 00:35:44.370
with a family.

00:35:44.370 --> 00:35:47.470
So there's some nice places in...

00:35:48.350 --> 00:35:50.320
Well, there's nice places everywhere

00:35:50.320 --> 00:35:55.270
but I definitely, San Diego
is now quite expensive too

00:35:55.270 --> 00:35:57.320
but it is cheaper than the Bay area

00:35:57.320 --> 00:35:59.010
but the Bay area is actually a great place

00:35:59.010 --> 00:36:00.350
for a lifelong learner.

00:36:00.350 --> 00:36:01.930
The cost of living is very high

00:36:01.930 --> 00:36:05.180
but as I say, your backyard
because the weather is good.

00:36:05.180 --> 00:36:07.460
You can do that as one of
the rooms of your house.

00:36:07.460 --> 00:36:09.983
So, that makes it a little bit better.

00:36:11.000 --> 00:36:12.250
Yeah, there's a lot of places,

00:36:12.250 --> 00:36:13.930
depends what you look for.

00:36:13.930 --> 00:36:17.214
For me, it's really who
you surround yourself with.

00:36:17.214 --> 00:36:19.360
Do you have a good friend,
friendships, community?

00:36:19.360 --> 00:36:20.193
Are there other people

00:36:20.193 --> 00:36:22.210
who intellectually stimulate you around?

00:36:22.210 --> 00:36:26.350
If you have that, almost anywhere
can be pretty incredible.

00:36:26.350 --> 00:36:28.873
So, let's see.

00:36:31.830 --> 00:36:36.270
I guess, let's see are
there any other questions?

00:36:36.270 --> 00:36:37.103
Well, there's so many,

00:36:37.103 --> 00:36:38.150
I feel like going through all of this

00:36:38.150 --> 00:36:42.050
but I'm guessing I'm
missing a meeting right now.

00:36:42.050 --> 00:36:43.650
I've been guilty of that before.

00:36:45.460 --> 00:36:46.967
Let's see, Sammy Sultana,

00:36:46.967 --> 00:36:48.340
"What inspired you to study at MIT?"

00:36:48.340 --> 00:36:49.940
Well, I don't wanna
make this a big MIT ad.

00:36:49.940 --> 00:36:52.970
I think there's many great universities

00:36:52.970 --> 00:36:54.980
but it was, I'll tell you the story.

00:36:54.980 --> 00:36:56.810
When I was seven years old,

00:36:56.810 --> 00:37:01.460
my mother's second to youngest brother,

00:37:01.460 --> 00:37:03.990
so my uncle at the time,

00:37:03.990 --> 00:37:07.480
who we were living with him in a while

00:37:07.480 --> 00:37:08.550
but anyway, we were at his house.

00:37:08.550 --> 00:37:10.200
I remember this, I was seven years old

00:37:10.200 --> 00:37:12.930
and he was in college at the
university of New Orleans.

00:37:12.930 --> 00:37:15.080
And I remember him doing some math

00:37:15.080 --> 00:37:15.970
and I remember being intrigued

00:37:15.970 --> 00:37:17.730
'cause it looked just
like random symbols to me.

00:37:17.730 --> 00:37:19.120
I was like, his name is Dal Shadmamu.

00:37:19.120 --> 00:37:23.090
Mamu in our language means
uncle, mother's brother.

00:37:23.090 --> 00:37:25.450
So I said, "Dal Shadmamu, what is this?"

00:37:25.450 --> 00:37:27.320
And he said, "Sal, this is calculus,"

00:37:27.320 --> 00:37:29.580
and I was like, "What is calculus?"

00:37:29.580 --> 00:37:31.500
And he's like, "Well,
it's this math where..."

00:37:31.500 --> 00:37:33.167
He tried to explain and then I said,

00:37:33.167 --> 00:37:34.990
"Can you teach me calculus?"

00:37:34.990 --> 00:37:36.797
And he's like, "Well, no, I don't know

00:37:36.797 --> 00:37:38.000
"if I can teach you calculus,"

00:37:38.000 --> 00:37:40.170
and I was like, "Why do you
need to learn calculus?"

00:37:40.170 --> 00:37:42.240
And he's like, "Well, I'm
studying to be an engineer,"

00:37:42.240 --> 00:37:44.940
and I said, and I remember
this super vividly.

00:37:44.940 --> 00:37:47.500
And I said, "Well, what
does an engineer do?"

00:37:47.500 --> 00:37:49.887
And he's like, "Well, an
engineer builds things

00:37:49.887 --> 00:37:51.790
"to solve problems in the world."

00:37:51.790 --> 00:37:53.990
Like he wanted to become
a mechanical engineer,

00:37:53.990 --> 00:37:57.050
and then I said, "Well, then
I wanna be an engineer too,"

00:37:57.050 --> 00:37:58.557
and then he says, "Well, if
you wanna be an engineer,

00:37:58.557 --> 00:38:00.030
"you should go to MIT,"

00:38:00.030 --> 00:38:01.860
and he doesn't remember.

00:38:01.860 --> 00:38:03.150
I've told him this story many times.

00:38:03.150 --> 00:38:05.220
He has no recollection of the story

00:38:05.220 --> 00:38:08.450
but I had no idea what MIT
was, what it stood for,

00:38:08.450 --> 00:38:11.810
where it was but something
just entered into my brain,

00:38:11.810 --> 00:38:14.610
based on what my uncle's
five-minute conversation

00:38:14.610 --> 00:38:15.443
with me when I was seven.

00:38:15.443 --> 00:38:17.757
I was like, "Oh, I should go there,"

00:38:19.371 --> 00:38:21.963
and I remember in high school,

00:38:22.870 --> 00:38:24.510
in ninth or 10th grade,

00:38:24.510 --> 00:38:26.430
it just kind of stayed
in the back of my mind

00:38:26.430 --> 00:38:28.580
and then when I talked to the
guidance counselor about it,

00:38:28.580 --> 00:38:29.557
the guidance counselor's like,

00:38:29.557 --> 00:38:31.730
"Sal, you gotta up your
game a little bit."

00:38:31.730 --> 00:38:34.510
Not that I was not a good student

00:38:34.510 --> 00:38:37.590
but I grew up in Louisiana

00:38:37.590 --> 00:38:39.670
and there's probably about
five kids a year who go

00:38:39.670 --> 00:38:42.150
and I didn't go to one of the
famous schools in the state.

00:38:42.150 --> 00:38:45.630
And so, anyway, I was always
enamored by the notion,

00:38:45.630 --> 00:38:48.333
and for me personally,
it was wonderful place.

00:38:49.750 --> 00:38:51.960
There's a lot of, it's a place...

00:38:51.960 --> 00:38:54.840
I gave a commencement address
at MIT a couple of years ago

00:38:54.840 --> 00:38:56.810
and I said, it's the
closest thing I can think of

00:38:56.810 --> 00:38:58.750
on the planet to like Hogwarts,

00:38:58.750 --> 00:39:02.400
where you can debate
whether there's real magic

00:39:02.400 --> 00:39:05.700
in the world, but a place
where people are focused on

00:39:05.700 --> 00:39:09.490
creating cool things
that could help humanity

00:39:09.490 --> 00:39:10.677
and are creative about it

00:39:10.677 --> 00:39:14.050
and are very accepting of
people's eccentricities.

00:39:14.050 --> 00:39:15.520
I thought was really nice

00:39:15.520 --> 00:39:17.410
and I've obviously made
some of my best friends

00:39:17.410 --> 00:39:19.610
from college and I met my wife there.

00:39:19.610 --> 00:39:23.020
So I definitely, I had a good experience

00:39:23.020 --> 00:39:25.103
but obviously I think any experience,

00:39:26.290 --> 00:39:28.600
one thing I've learned in life is,

00:39:28.600 --> 00:39:30.030
it's not where you go,

00:39:30.030 --> 00:39:33.110
it's not all of these things

00:39:33.110 --> 00:39:34.330
we often put a lot of importance on,

00:39:34.330 --> 00:39:36.600
it's the energy that you project outward.

00:39:36.600 --> 00:39:38.460
So if you go, wherever you go to college,

00:39:38.460 --> 00:39:40.837
if you say, "Hey, I am so lucky.

00:39:40.837 --> 00:39:42.097
"I get to be in an environment

00:39:42.097 --> 00:39:43.667
"that is focused on learning,

00:39:43.667 --> 00:39:45.677
"an environment where I can meet friends

00:39:45.677 --> 00:39:47.710
"and bond and be open."

00:39:47.710 --> 00:39:49.400
If you put that energy out there,

00:39:49.400 --> 00:39:50.610
the friendships will happen.

00:39:50.610 --> 00:39:52.530
You will learn, you will explore.

00:39:52.530 --> 00:39:54.470
People will gravitate to you.

00:39:54.470 --> 00:39:56.400
But if you go with kind
of a cynical attitude

00:39:56.400 --> 00:39:57.233
and you're like,

00:39:57.233 --> 00:39:58.900
"Oh, these people are
out to compete with me,"

00:39:58.900 --> 00:40:01.810
or, "Oh, this isn't my
first choice school,"

00:40:01.810 --> 00:40:02.916
or things like that.

00:40:02.916 --> 00:40:05.390
Then it'll kind of be a
self-fulfilling prophecy.

00:40:05.390 --> 00:40:08.450
So I would say, regardless
of where you wanna go,

00:40:08.450 --> 00:40:09.750
obviously I only had
one college experience

00:40:09.750 --> 00:40:11.020
and that was MIT

00:40:11.020 --> 00:40:12.620
and I really enjoyed it.

00:40:12.620 --> 00:40:15.440
But what I found, whether
it's at MIT or other places,

00:40:15.440 --> 00:40:16.950
there were people who didn't
have a good experience

00:40:16.950 --> 00:40:18.060
and oftentimes it might've been

00:40:18.060 --> 00:40:21.310
because it came with some
cynicism to it or whatever.

00:40:21.310 --> 00:40:24.010
And so, that's my best advice.

00:40:24.010 --> 00:40:26.140
It's not going this school or that,

00:40:26.140 --> 00:40:29.140
it's the attitude that you bring to it.

00:40:29.140 --> 00:40:31.790
So we're all out of time.

00:40:31.790 --> 00:40:33.780
I'm enjoying hamming it
up with y'all right now

00:40:33.780 --> 00:40:35.220
and y'all are asking some
really good questions

00:40:35.220 --> 00:40:36.540
but we'll have more of these,

00:40:36.540 --> 00:40:39.760
Ask Sal Anything sessions in the future.

00:40:39.760 --> 00:40:41.890
So thanks a bunch for joining

00:40:41.890 --> 00:40:44.330
and everyone have a wonderful weekend.

00:40:44.330 --> 00:40:45.560
Thanks for joining the live stream.

00:40:45.560 --> 00:40:46.810
I'll see y'all on Monday.

